      "text": "The commercial fallout from Brazil's ban reached European and US-listed groups within days. Entain, which operates Sportingbet in Brazil, cut its full-year online net gaming revenue growth outlook to 4-6% and now expects earnings at the lower end of its GBP 910m-960m range; Brazil had been expected to contribute about 5% of online revenue, and its shares fell 4%. Flutter estimated that a shutdown for the rest of 2026 would remove about USD 70m of revenue and USD 20m of EBITDA, and disclosed that its Brazil business carries USD 539m of goodwill, USD 127m of customer relationships and USD 124m of trademarks. Flutter said it was 'extremely disappointed' and was 'reviewing all available options, including the potential to appeal'; Entain said the measure was made 'without consultation of industry stakeholders regarding its significant adverse consequences'. Yogonet separately reported on 30 September that Allwyn's roughly 37% adjusted EBITDA margin guidance will no longer apply if the ban lasts through year-end; Allwyn owns 36.75% of Kaizen Gaming, whose Betano brand is preparing legal action. The near-term P&L hits look modest for Flutter and Entain, but the goodwill and intangibles disclosures are the bigger story: they put a price on what a regulated-market licence is worth when it can be withdrawn by decree, and that number will now feature in every board discussion about market-entry capex, local tech builds and B2B commitments in jurisdictions with volatile politics.",
