      "title": "CFTC sends two event-contract rules to the White House, one excluding 'casino-style' products via an interim final rule",
      "text": "The Commodity Futures Trading Commission has filed two proposals with the Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs at the White House Office of Management and Budget, The Block reported on 30 September. One would 'further' the definition of a swap to include event contracts; the other would 'exclude casino-style gambling products' from that definition, and OIRA lists it at the interim final rule stage. Gaming lawyer Daniel Wallach noted that an interim final rule takes effect immediately without notice-and-comment, exposing it to Administrative Procedure Act challenge: 'If the interim rule goes beyond the title's suggestion (e.g., excluding casino-style products) and provides tacit authorization for sports-event contracts, it could prompt immediate APA litigation in federal court.' The filing comes days after the Sixth Circuit sided with Ohio and Tennessee against Kalshi on 25 September, and after New Jersey Attorney General Jennifer Davenport asked the Supreme Court to resolve who regulates sports event contracts. The CFTC has also filed countersuits to block state oversight. This is the first concrete sign of how the federal regulator intends to draw the line: casino-style products out, sports and events potentially in. If that holds, the competitive threat to licensed operators narrows to sports and novelty markets rather than iGaming, but it would also crystallise a federally-sanctioned sports channel that bypasses state tax, age-21 and responsible-gambling rules, which is exactly what the AGA spent G2E campaigning against.",
