      "text": "The Betting and Gaming Council launched its 'Back Our Betting Shops' campaign on 28 September, featuring staff, apprentices and customers and positioning shops as community hubs, in opposition to a proposed rise in Machine Games Duty to 40%. The BGC cites EY modelling that the increase could put up to 16,000 jobs, nearly 1,500 betting shops and up to 34 casinos at risk while leaving the Treasury GBP 124m worse off. The campaign is targeted at Makerfield, the prime minister's constituency, where an Opinium poll found 54% of residents said betting shops add to local community life. Entain CEO Stella David warned against doubling MGD, and Betfred's Fred Done said the rise would force the company to close 495 shops within a year, costing 2,575 jobs and around GBP 67m in tax; Betfred has already closed 132 shops this year. BGC chief executive Grainne Hurst said: 'Behind every betting shop is a team of real people earning a living, supporting their families and playing a part in their local community.' The MGD fight follows April's Remote Gaming Duty rise to 40% and the scheduled General Betting Duty increase to 25% in April 2027. Taken together, the UK is converging on a tax structure where both retail machines and online casino carry a 40% rate, which compresses the margin available for bonusing, CRM and acquisition across every channel and makes omnichannel wallet and loyalty economics harder to justify for GB-facing operators.",
