      "text": "Alongside the provisional measure, Brazil's Executive sent bill PL 5.477/2026 to the Chamber of Deputies on 25 September and requested urgent processing on 28 September, Yogonet reported. Because Article 62 of the constitution bars provisional measures on criminal law, the new offences need an ordinary bill. Operating fixed-odds betting or running its sites and apps would carry four to six years' imprisonment plus a fine. Two to four years would apply to advertising or recruiting bettors, to using personal data to target bettors, to processing, settling or holding payments knowing their purpose, and to supplying, licensing, hosting or maintaining betting platforms or software knowing their purpose. Penalties increase for casino-style games such as slots and where payment flows are disguised or routed abroad; journalistic and academic content is exempt. The government's justification is that the offences let it 'confront the different means employed to sustain the activity, including in the digital environment'. This is the clause B2B suppliers need to read closely. Platform, game, hosting and payment providers have never before been directly named as potential criminal defendants in a major market; if the bill passes in this form, continuing to serve any Brazilian-facing brand, including through white-label or aggregation chains, becomes a personal-liability question for executives rather than a commercial or contractual one.",
