    "section": "Europe",
    "title": "Norway proposes letting Norsk Tipping offer online poker for the first time, with hard loss caps and break rules",
    "meta": "iGaming Business | 30 Sep 2026",
    "summary": "Norway's Ministry of Culture and Equality published a fast-track consultation on 29 September that would allow state-owned Norsk Tipping to offer online tournament poker and cash games. Proposed limits include loss caps of NOK 5,000 a day and NOK 10,000 a month (NOK 2,000 a month for under-20s), tournament buy-ins capped at NOK 2,500 and cash-game buy-ins at NOK 1,000, a mandatory five-minute break each hour in tournaments, a four-table maximum and a 15-minute pause when switching between poker and casino. The largest operators would also have to report serious incidents to Lotteritilsynet.",
    "why": "Norway is one of the last committed monopoly markets in Europe, and this is a monopoly conceding that an unregulated vertical has grown too large to ignore: a 2022 University of Bergen survey put offshore online poker players at nearly 90,000 a year, and Norsk Tipping's own estimate is 100,000 to 150,000. The design choices tell you how Nordic regulators think about cross-vertical harm. A mandatory cooling-off period when a player moves between poker and casino is a session-state rule that has to be enforced in the wallet and game-launch layer, not in the front end, and it is the kind of control other Nordic and Baltic regulators tend to borrow.",
    "expanded": "Culture Minister Lubna Jaffery said: 'Many Norwegians play online poker. It is better that we get a responsible and safer offer here in Norway, rather than unregulated games at foreign, commercial gaming companies.' The proposal is framed explicitly as channelisation rather than liberalisation, so it does not open the door to private licences. The serious-incident reporting duty is a separate and potentially wider change, since it introduces an operational-resilience obligation familiar from financial services. Because liquidity is the core problem for a single-country poker room, watch whether the final rules allow Norsk Tipping to pool players with other Nordic state operators.",
    "source": "https://igamingbusiness.com/casino-games/poker/norway-proposes-online-poker-legislation-to-combat-unregulated-activity/",
