    "summary": "Austria's draft law would open applications for multiple online licences on 1 January 2027, with licences valid from 1 October 2027 when Win2Day's sole online licence expires. Operators still active in the market after 1 January would face an 18-month wait before applying. Speaking at SBC Summit Lisbon, lawyer Felix Hohenthanner of Rapani Rechtsanwälte and OVWG president Simon Priglinger-Simader called the reform a landmark but warned the timetable is risky. The two-week consultation drew more than 100 submissions, and the regulator that would run payment and IP blocking has not been created.",
    "why": "The 18-month cooling-off for operators still active after 1 January is the clause that forces real decisions. Grey-market operators serving Austria now have to choose between exiting a revenue stream for nine months with no guarantee of a licence, or staying and being locked out until 2028. Priglinger-Simader's warning that applicants could fall below 10 because player refund claims cannot be deducted from the tax base points to the Austrian-specific legacy issue: years of court-ordered player refunds against unlicensed operators are now a cost carried into the licensed era.",
