    "summary": "VNLOK, which represents licensed Dutch online operators, formally summoned Meta to court in Amsterdam on 28 September, claiming it has failed its duties under the EU Digital Services Act. VNLOK says more than 90% of gambling ads on Facebook come from operators without a Dutch licence, that Meta removed only 11% of flagged illegal ads in May and 15% in June, and that Meta platforms showed more than 70,000 gambling ads in Q4 2025, over 95% from unlicensed operators, of which Meta removed under 5%.",
    "why": "This is licensed operators using the DSA as a commercial weapon against the channel that most efficiently feeds the black market. In the Netherlands it matters more than elsewhere because licensed operators are themselves heavily restricted in untargeted advertising, so an unlicensed brand buying Facebook reach is competing on a field licensees cannot enter. If VNLOK wins, it creates a template for trade bodies in other EU markets to force platform-level takedown obligations, which would shift some enforcement cost from regulators and licensees to Big Tech.",
    "expanded": "A motion from VVD MP Bart Bikkers to give the Kansspelautoriteit explicit powers to fine platforms that ignore repeated notices was due for a vote on 29 September. VNLOK began proceedings in June and has also complained to the European Commission. Chairman Fuchs said: 'We have repeatedly pointed out the scale of the problem to Meta and asked them to cooperate structurally in finding a solution. That has yielded insufficient results.' He also described the whack-a-mole problem: 'If an illegal provider is removed via one platform, the same provider pops up again via another channel.' The case sits against a backdrop in which the SOFTSWISS trends report says the regulated Dutch share has fallen below 50%.",
