    "commentary": "Regulus wrote that 'by far the biggest winner of the disruption… will be the black market as all regular customers will be diverted there and will be very hard to bring back', and warned that 'the scale of industrial damage that such a blackout can do should not be underestimated'. On tax it said the revenue 'will not be recovered after a ban lasting more than a month or so'. Per iGB, it sees legal grounds for challenge because 'suddenly banning a product which is specifically and directly taxed clearly does threaten federal tax revenue and no legally required justification or mitigation has been made public'. It also raised a political risk: 'There is also a clear danger that a Lula victory followed by a PM defeat will lead to tighter gambling regulations of the performative sort that drive the black market.' And on operations: 'What licensees are supposed to do with their Brazil-facing staff and long-term contract liabilities has not been made clear.' The Racing Post note concludes: 'Performative politics wrecks lives and economies, as Lula da Silva seems keen to demonstrate.'",
